<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/foundational/global-prehistory-art-before-writing">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Art History, and it starts where art itself starts — before
    any written word existed. You need no prior knowledge of art, history or archaeology; if you
    have ever drawn an animal on a fogged window, you have already performed the basic act this
    lesson studies. The subject here is Art History's first survey unit: global prehistory,
    roughly 30,000 to 500 BCE, taught through the College Board's AP Art History framework.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what makes the study of art before writing different from all later art history, and why interpretation must stay disciplined;</li>
    <li>identify the eleven required prehistoric works — from the Apollo 11 stones to the Lapita terra cotta fragment — by culture, medium and approximate date;</li>
    <li>explain how environment, migration, hunting and the shift to farming shaped what prehistoric people made (topic 1.1);</li>
    <li>describe the materials, processes and techniques — pigment, engraving, ceramics, megalith construction — and what each demanded (topic 1.2);</li>
    <li>state the major theories of why prehistoric art was made, and what evidence supports and limits each (topic 1.3).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A note on method before anything else: every date in this lesson is "c." — circa, about.
    Nothing here was ever written down by its makers.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea: art history without a single sentence from the artists</h2>
  <p>
    Nearly every other unit of art history is a conversation. The artist wrote letters, patrons
    signed contracts, critics argued in print. Prehistory is different: the entire record is
    objects. For tens of thousands of years before writing (invented around 3300 BCE in
    Mesopotamia, and not everywhere at once), humans painted caves, carved bone and stone, fired
    clay and raised megaliths — and left no explanation. That silence is the defining condition
    of this unit, and it shapes everything: the dating must come from the materials themselves,
    the context from the ground layers, and the meaning from careful, testable inference. The
    reward for that discipline is the longest-running chapter of the human story: art is not a
    late luxury of civilisation. It comes first — figurative images tens of thousands of years
    older than the first alphabet, monumental architecture older than the first city. Whoever
    made these things was fully us.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> prehistoric art history means reading objects alone, in silence,
    across a span longer than all written history combined. The first question to ask of any
    object is the one the syllabus calls cultural influence: what world was its maker living in?
  </p>

  <h2>Cultural influences: the world the makers lived in (1.1)</h2>
  <p>
    For most of this period, humans everywhere lived by hunting and gathering, moving with the
    herds and the seasons, and the climate itself was a character: ice sheets locked up so much
    water that coastlines, rainfall zones and whole biomes sat somewhere else. Two consequences
    follow for art. First, mobile life meant small, portable or site-bound art — a carved slab
    you can carry, or a painted cave you return to; there were no cities, temples or
    workshops yet. Second, the natural world — animals above all — supplied the subjects,
    because animals were food, danger and spiritual neighbour at once. The great shift arrives
    with the <strong>Neolithic</strong> transition, from around 9000 BCE in some regions:
    people begin farming, herding, and settling. Everything in the art record changes with it.
    Settled life can accumulate things, so we get pottery (the Susa beaker, the Lapita ware),
    larger settlements, craft specialists (the jade cong), and finally monumental, communal
    architecture (Stonehenge) that a village of farmers could only raise by organising labour
    beyond the family. The Running horned woman captures the climate side of the story from the
    other end: it was painted on a rock shelter in a green savanna with rivers and herds, in
    what is now the dead centre of the Sahara — the desert arrived later, and the art stayed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notice what this topic is really claiming: art does not float free. Where people live, what
    they eat, how they move and how many of them can stop working to make things — those facts
    set the menu of what can be made. This is the first appearance of a habit you will use in
    every later unit: read the society through the object.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "prehistoric people were primitive, so their art must be
  crude child-drawings." It seems reasonable because the images are simple outlines and the
  makers had no metal, wheel or writing. But the Great Hall of the Bulls shows observed
  anatomy, overlapping perspective and pigments engineered to last millennia — a Lascaux
  painter mixed minerals with binders and applied them with tools we still cannot fully
  reconstruct; the camelid sacrum turns a pelvis into a dog by reading the bone's own shape.
  The correct picture: the makers were modern humans with the same brains as you, solving
  hard problems with the materials at hand; simplicity is a style choice, not a stage of
  intelligence. Contrast the jade cong: no "primitive" hand can grind jade — it takes skill,
  patience and social surplus.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the makers are invisible, so the evidence is all — climate and
    migration set where art could happen, and every surviving object is a rare survivor of
    what was almost certainly a much larger practice.
  </p>

  <h2>Materials, processes and techniques (1.2)</h2>
  <p>
    Prehistoric artists were also chemists and engineers, and the syllabus asks you to know the
    techniques. <strong>Painting:</strong> the pigments are minerals — charcoal and manganese
    for black, ochre (iron oxide) for red and yellow — ground to powder and mixed with a binder
    such as animal fat, blood or plant juice; at Lascaux some pigment was blown through tubes
    or directly by mouth, leaving spray effects, and surfaces were prepared so paint gripped.
    Deep caves were worked by stone-lamp light. <strong>Carving:</strong> the Apollo 11 stones
    and the camelid sacrum are engraved or carved with stone tools; the Ambum Stone is ground
    from greywacke, an extremely hard stone, which means weeks or months of patient abrasion;
    jade is harder still — the Liangzhu cong was shaped by grinding with sand-and-cord or
    sand-and-bamboo over what must have been a very long production, which is the whole point
    of jade as a material: it converts time and skill into value. <strong>Ceramics:</strong> the
    Susa bushel and the Lapita fragment are fired clay; the Lapita makers stamped wet clay with
    small toothed combs, building repeated patterns — dentate stamping — a technique that
    travelled with them across the Pacific. <strong>Megalithic construction:</strong> Stonehenge
    is the extreme case — stones weighing many tonnes, dressed with stone hammers, dragged on
    sledges and rollers, and raised using levers and earthen ramps; the smaller bluestones were
    hauled from Wales, roughly 150 miles away, which tells you the organising power behind the
    monument. Every technique on this list answers the same two questions: what did the
    material allow, and what did it cost in human effort?
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "cave painters just drew with their hands using charcoal
  sticks, like a child with a crayon." It seems reasonable because the images look loose and
  the tools sound simple. But analysis of the paintings shows prepared surfaces, mixed
  pigments with binders, blown sprays, stencilled hands, scaffolding marks high on walls, and
  deliberate reuse of the rock's bumps to give animals volume — plus the logistics of light
  and air hundreds of metres underground. The correct picture: this is a trained technology,
  learned and transmitted, with division of labour between pigment-makers and painters. The
  contrasting example is the Lapita stamping: repeated motifs require a manufactured tool and
  a compositional system — the opposite of improvised doodling.</p>

  <h2>The works, in one sweep</h2>
  <p>
    Now put names, places and dates together. Scrub the timeline below from the Apollo 11
    stones of Namibia (c. 25,500 BCE — the cave got its name because the moon landing happened
    during the excavation) through the Great Hall of the Bulls at Lascaux (c. 15,000–13,000
    BCE, five-metre bulls deep inside a French hillside), across the Pacific and the Americas
    — the camelid sacrum of central Mexico (c. 7000 BCE), the Ambum Stone of New Guinea, the
    two-faced ceramics of Tlatilco, the stamped faces of Lapita — through the agricultural
    societies of Asia (the Susa bushel, the Arabian stele, the jade cong of Liangzhu) to the
    stone circle of Stonehenge (c. 2500–1600 BCE).
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="eleven required works, 25,000 years — from charcoal on stone to stones on end" widget="timeline" config={prehistTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the spans, not the order. Between the first and last
    event, twenty-four millennia pass — every dynasty you have ever heard of fits in the final
    tenth of this timeline. Notice also the pattern of tags: figuration (Apollo 11, Lascaux)
    comes first everywhere, then objects for settled societies (ceramics, jade), then
    monumental communal works (Stonehenge). Same species, same sequence, four continents — that
    recurrence is itself evidence about human nature, and it is why this unit is global rather
    than a tour of Europe's caves.
  </p>

  <h2>Theories and interpretations: why did they make it? (1.3)</h2>
  <p>
    With no texts, the why must be argued, and the arguments have names. <strong>Art for art's
    sake:</strong> the oldest view — people decorate; it is pleasure. It fits the beauty of the
    works but not the setting: who paints masterpieces in freezing, dangerous caves that no one
    lives in? <strong>Hunting and sympathetic magic:</strong> the abbé Henri Breuil's early
    twentieth-century case — painting an animal is a ritual act that helps capture it; the
    wounded-bull and dart-mark imagery fits, but many painted species were not eaten, which
    weakens the pure hunting reading. <strong>Structuralism:</strong> André Leroi-Gourhan in the
    1950s and 60s mapped whole cave systems and found the pairings and placements too
    consistent to be casual — the cave as a designed sanctuary, images composed by rules, not
    accumulated by accident. <strong>Shamanism and neuropsychology:</strong> David
    Lewis-Williams's influential model — the geometric signs (dots, grids, zigzags) match the
    entoptic patterns the human visual system produces in trance, and deep caves were stages
    for ritual specialists negotiating a spirit world; the model unifies geometry, animals and
    location, which is why it dominates current teaching, while remaining unprovable. For
    later, settled works, the readings shift: the jade cong's square-earth, round-heaven form
    and the solstice alignments of Stonehenge point to ritual, cosmology and community
    identity rather than hunting. The skill the syllabus actually wants is the habit: state the
    theory, name the evidence for it, and name what would have to be true for it to be wrong.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "archaeologists have proved the cave paintings were about
  hunting magic." It seems reasonable because the theory is old, famous and intuitive — paint
  the hunt, get the hunt. But the evidence is circumstantial: many painted species were not
  staple foods, some images sit where no hunter would need them, and the geometric signs fit
  no hunting logic at all. The correct picture: all theories on offer are interpretations to
  be weighed, not results to be memorised; the strongest current one (shamanic trance) explains
  the most evidence but remains a hypothesis. A contrasting example: Göbekli Tepe in Turkey,
  a monumental carved complex built around 9600 BCE by people who had not yet farmed — found
  in 1994 and excavated from 1995, it overturned the tidy story that monumentality waited for
  agriculture, and it did so by being dug up, not by anyone's theory winning an argument.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name three of the required works, each from a different continent, with its medium and approximate date.</li>
    <li>The Neolithic shift changed both what was made and who made it. Give one change in art forms and one change in social organisation that the art record implies.</li>
    <li>State two theories of why deep-cave art was made, with one piece of supporting evidence and one weakness for each.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Examples: Apollo 11 stones — charcoal on stone, Namibia,
    c. 25,500 BCE; Running horned woman — pigment on rock, Algeria, c. 6000–4000 BCE; jade
    cong — carved jade, Liangzhu, China, c. 3300–2200 BCE; the camelid sacrum (Mexico, c. 7000
    BCE), Ambum Stone (Papua New Guinea, c. 1500 BCE), Tlatilco figurine (Mexico, c. 1200–900
    BCE) and Lapita fragment (Solomon Islands, c. 1000 BCE) also qualify. (2) Art forms:
    pottery and stamped ceramics appear (Susa, Lapita), plus monumental communal architecture
    (Stonehenge). Social organisation: surplus food supports craft specialists (jade grinding
    requires weeks of one person's time) and the coordinated labour of megalith building —
    hierarchy and planning beyond the family. (3) Hunting/sympathetic magic — supported by
    dart-marked animals, weakened by non-food species being painted; shamanic/neuropsychological
    — supported by entoptic geometric signs matching trance patterns worldwide and by the
    liminal location of deep caves, weakened by being untestable for any specific cave.
    Structuralism and art-for-art's-sake are acceptable alternatives if argued with evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Prehistory means reading objects in silence: no texts from the makers, so dating comes from materials and context, meaning from disciplined inference.</li>
    <li>Art precedes civilisation: figurative images and monuments are older than writing, cities and farming — the makers were modern humans like us.</li>
    <li>Environment drives content: Ice Age hunters paint herds; the green Sahara leaves the Running horned woman; farmers build ceramics, jade and megaliths.</li>
    <li>The techniques are engineered: ground mineral pigments with binders, blown sprays, stone-tool carving, abrasive jade grinding, comb-stamped pottery, hauled multi-tonne sarsens.</li>
    <li>Know the eleven works by name, place, medium and date — from Apollo 11 (c. 25,500 BCE) to Lapita (c. 1000 BCE).</li>
    <li>Theories — art for art's sake, hunting magic, structuralism, shamanism — are interpretations to weigh, each with evidence and limits; the strongest explains the most, none is proved.</li>
    <li>Attribution habit for every later unit: what did the material allow, what did it cost in effort, and what does that imply about the society?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The survey continues in <em>Walk Through Western Art</em>, the foundational lesson that
    carries you from Giotto to Warhol with the same read-the-society-through-the-object
    method; <em>Art Beyond the West</em> widens the map to the non-Western traditions this
    global prehistory already belongs to. Later units will reuse this lesson's core skill —
    when the Renaissance workshop or the Impressionist studio appears, you will recognise the
    same chain of patron, material and labour you met in the jade cong.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    How do we actually know the dates? The deepest technical layer of this unit is dating
    method, because every claim above rests on it. <strong>Radiocarbon dating</strong> measures
    the decay of carbon-14 in once-living material — the charcoal of a torch splutter, a bone
    fragment on a cave floor — good to roughly 50,000 years back, with a margin of centuries
    to millennia. Where no organic matter touches the art, <strong>uranium-series dating</strong>
    helps in a clever, indirect way: calcite crusts that form over a painting contain uranium
    but little thorium, so the crust's uranium decay dates the crust — which brackets the
    painting's age (the art must be older than the film that covers it). Cross-checks come
    from stratigraphy — the depth order of deposits — and from comparing styles across sites.
    When three independent methods agree, a "c. 25,500 BCE" stops being a guess and becomes a
    constrained result with an error bar — which is the closest thing prehistory has to a
    citation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other deep layer is conservation, and Lascaux is its defining cautionary tale. Opened
    to tourists after its 1940 discovery, the cave received more than a thousand visitors a
    day; the carbon dioxide and humidity of human breath, plus the lighting installed for
    them, triggered moulds and calcium deposits — researchers named the white bloom "la
    maladie blanche" — and by 1963 the cave was closed for good; today even researchers enter
    rarely, under strict climate control, and visitors see full-scale replicas. The lesson for
    the whole discipline: prehistoric art survives by exact accident — the sealed, stable
    climate of a deep cave — and the moment of discovery is also the moment the clock starts
    running. Every cave you will ever read about in this course is now either closed, gated or
    climate-monitored; the archaeology of the twenty-first century is as much about keeping
    what is found as finding it.
  </p>

